# .checkovignore
# Accepted Checkov findings for stage-2-ecs-fargate/infra/
# Format: CHECK_ID # Justification
#
# Each entry documents WHY the finding is accepted rather than remediated.
# Review this file when findings are resolved upstream.

# CKV_AWS_274: IAM policy uses wildcard resource
# ecr:GetAuthorizationToken is an AWS API that does not support resource-level
# permissions. The token is account-wide by design. This is an AWS constraint,
# not a misconfiguration. Affects: task execution role, GitHub Actions role.
CKV_AWS_274

# CKV_AWS_91: ALB access logging not enabled
# Access logs require a dedicated S3 bucket with a specific bucket policy.
# Out of scope for this development deployment. Enable for production.
CKV_AWS_91

# CKV2_AWS_57: Secrets Manager secret does not have automatic rotation
# The app-config secret holds application configuration values, not credentials
# with a known rotation mechanism (e.g. RDS passwords). Rotation is a manual
# process for this secret type. For database credentials, use an AWS-managed
# rotation Lambda via aws_secretsmanager_secret_rotation.
CKV2_AWS_57

# CKV_AWS_150: ALB deletion protection not enabled
# Deletion protection is appropriate for production load balancers.
# Disabled here to allow clean teardown of development infrastructure
# without manual intervention.
CKV_AWS_150

# CKV_AWS_338: CloudWatch log group retention less than 1 year
# 30-day retention on the ECS log group and 90-day retention on VPC flow logs
# are appropriate for a development deployment. Production workloads subject
# to compliance requirements should use 365 days or longer.
CKV_AWS_338

# CKV_AWS_290: IAM policy allows write access without constraints
# CKV_AWS_355: IAM policy uses wildcard resource for restrictable actions
# The GitHub Actions deployment role requires ecs:RegisterTaskDefinition and
# ecs:DescribeServices with Resource = "*" — AWS does not support resource-level
# permissions for these ECS API actions. This is an AWS constraint.
CKV_AWS_290
CKV_AWS_355

# CKV_AWS_382: Security group allows unrestricted egress
# The ALB security group requires unrestricted egress to forward traffic to
# ECS tasks across the private subnet CIDR. The ECS tasks security group
# requires unrestricted egress to reach VPC endpoints and the NAT gateway
# for outbound traffic. Scoping egress to specific CIDRs would require
# hardcoding subnet ranges that are already parameterized as variables.
CKV_AWS_382

# CKV_AWS_260: Security group allows ingress from 0.0.0.0/0 on port 80
# Port 80 ingress is required on the ALB security group to receive HTTP
# requests and issue a 301 redirect to HTTPS. Blocking port 80 entirely
# would prevent the redirect and break the user experience for HTTP clients.
CKV_AWS_260

# CKV2_AWS_12: VPC default security group does not restrict all traffic
# Remediating this requires an explicit aws_default_security_group resource
# to override the AWS-managed default. Deferred for development scope.
# Add aws_default_security_group with no ingress/egress rules for production.
CKV2_AWS_12

# CKV2_AWS_28: Public-facing ALB not protected by WAF
# WAF adds meaningful cost and operational overhead. Appropriate for
# production workloads. Out of scope for this development deployment.
CKV2_AWS_28

# CKV2_AWS_3: GuardDuty not enabled to specific org/region
# This check targets multi-account AWS Organizations deployments where
# GuardDuty is delegated to a central security account. This is a
# single-account personal project. GuardDuty is enabled and Runtime
# Monitoring is active. The finding does not apply to this architecture.
CKV2_AWS_3

# CKV2_AWS_64: KMS key policy not explicitly defined
# Applies to the ECR and Secrets Manager KMS keys. AWS default key policy
# grants account root full access and relies on IAM for all other access
# control. Access to these keys is governed by the task execution role
# and GitHub Actions role policies. An explicit key policy would be
# redundant without adding meaningful security benefit in this context.
CKV2_AWS_64